  "author": "kanada",
  "attributionNote": "Traditionally ascribed to the sage Kanada, whose name means atom-eater. The extant sutra is a compilation, and its ten books grew over time.",
  "description": "The Vaisheshika Sutra sets out a philosophy of nature under six categories of reality: substance, quality, action, the universal, the particular and inherence. Within substance it argues that the material world is built from eternal atoms combined by an unseen force, and it joins that atomism to a theory of knowledge and to a religious account of duty and liberation.",
  "historicalContext": "With the Nyaya school it became the Nyaya-Vaisheshika tradition, whose categories and atomism were debated for a thousand years and which became the reference point for Indian logic and philosophy of nature.",
